WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.003
- [Anthony] Hello.

00:00:01.003 --> 00:00:02.872
- Hello, it's Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:00:02.872 --> 00:00:04.455
- [Group] Gary Vee!

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.270
- [Anthony] So Gary, really
quick, this is kinda like

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.960
destiny coming to fruition because

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.460
when I was in Pittsburgh,
when you had Crush It!

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.150
Originally the first one,

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:19.270
I set up and interview and you called in

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:21.130
and I was sick that day.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:24.280
And the board op at the station called me

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:25.340
later in the day and goes,

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.910
you have some guy, Gary, on the hotline?

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:28.852
Who's Gary?

00:00:28.852 --> 00:00:30.276
(laughs)

00:00:30.276 --> 00:00:33.460
And I missed the interview
and I was so embarrassed,

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.080
I didn't even try to reschedule with you.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:35.913
- I love it, man.

00:00:35.913 --> 00:00:38.040
Don't worry at all, this
makes me super happy,

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.330
let's rock 'n' roll.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.060
- [Anthony] Alright, so here's my deal.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.960
We know about the book,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.570
people are listening to this podcast

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.870
know about the book
because we're fans, right?

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.730
So I just want to go straight to Q&amp;A

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:50.680
- Love it.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.040
- [Anthony] Getting
some questions for you,

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.200
and let's just roll, okay?

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.600
- Love it, thank you.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.135
- Alright, so, you are so in tap right now

00:00:58.135 --> 00:00:59.937
with music, right?

00:00:59.937 --> 00:01:01.759
You got a ton of artists that come in

00:01:01.759 --> 00:01:06.270
for #AskGaryVee, come in
for DailyVee all the time.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.773
When are you gonna start
being in artist management?

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.210
- Probably never.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.840
You know, obviously, I'm
paying close attention

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.130
more than ever, it was
something that I was kind of

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.390
subtley into through the last 25 years,

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.274
then a little bit more into

00:01:23.274 --> 00:01:26.370
and but it's always been a part of my life

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.040
but not one that I
would talk about because

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.490
I didn't know it as well
as business and sports.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.920
But it was always there.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.436
Over the last couple years,
especially the last 18 months,

00:01:34.436 --> 00:01:37.330
to your point, I'm getting
real, real, real deep,

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.342
I think I'm bringing a lot of value.

00:01:39.342 --> 00:01:43.580
I would be lying if I
didn't say that management

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.240
or creating a label has
run through my mind,

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.550
I'm an entrepreneur, everything
runs through my mind.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.073
My intuition is, I won't do either.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.770
But I also would be
shocked if I'm not doing

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.594
something in the music
industry over the next

00:01:57.594 --> 00:01:59.660
three to five years.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.880
My intuition is it's
gonna be different, right?

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.942
I got into the liquor store business,

00:02:03.942 --> 00:02:07.970
but I did it by doing a e-commerce site

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:09.660
when everybody thought
the internet was crazy.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.050
I got into the Madison
Avenue advertising world

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.590
but I did it by only selling social media

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.810
which was unsellable at the time.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:21.530
So I think if I was to do
something actually official,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:25.650
other than trying to bring
value to people that I like

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.134
who I think are gonna be big stars,

00:02:28.134 --> 00:02:30.640
I think it's gonna come in a form

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.540
that people aren't expecting.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:34.554
- [Anthony] Okay.

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:38.070
You mentioned a lot,
especially in your keynotes

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:41.150
and in your DailyVee's that basically

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:45.640
radio is now where TV was
20 or 30 years ago, right?

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.233
So, with that,

00:02:48.512 --> 00:02:50.350
to me, as a radio personality,

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:52.270
'cause I watch all of your stuff,

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:53.890
like, religiously, okay?

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.920
And I haven't noticed a lot of guys,

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.760
I've seen you on The
Breakfast Club with Char,

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.400
I've seen you with Elvis Duran,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.930
But those guys were interviewing you,

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:05.370
rather than asking you
questions specifically

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.444
about the industry that we're in.

00:03:07.444 --> 00:03:08.277
- Yes.

00:03:08.277 --> 00:03:10.180
- [Anthony] So, given the fact that

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.240
we have to reinvent ourselves,

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.550
what would someone like me do

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.630
to stay relevant in an era where

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:18.410
voice is gonna become king?

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:19.490
- It's actually very easy.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.960
As long as you film yourself, or can,

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:24.850
or control it or not control it.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.410
But to me, if I'm a radio personality,

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.430
and I have full control, not the station,

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:32.410
or I'm aligned with the station,

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:35.000
to me it's just having a podcast

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.250
and just filming the content

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.980
and putting it on YouTube and Facebook

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.784
and literally you're as
relevant as anybody else.

00:03:42.784 --> 00:03:47.500
I don't see radio as this major negative,

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:50.890
as long as you also
vlog and have a podcast

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:52.950
and make social media content.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:53.783
Period.

00:03:53.783 --> 00:03:54.650
That's it.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:55.733
- [Anthony] Right.

00:03:55.733 --> 00:03:57.460
- So I would actually argue being in radio

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.630
is a huge advantage right now,

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:02.590
if you have the ability to film and also

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:04.850
create a podcast out of it.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:06.030
- [Anthony] Well that's funny because

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:07.350
I have a podcast, right?

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.430
It's like, oh man, this podcast was fire.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:10.840
It's gonna get a lot of listens, right?

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:12.820
'Cause I do have an advantage over

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:13.653
- Yep.

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:14.486
Joe Schmo.

00:04:14.486 --> 00:04:15.320
Yep.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.100
- [Anthony] I've done
it for my whole career.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.220
But it's tough because

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.690
I'll even play a clip
of a podcast on the air,

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:22.860
and be like, yo, you have to check out

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:24.480
the rest of the conversation.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.660
And there's a hoop I'm making
people jump through, right?

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.480
So, that's the hard thing was like,

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:30.740
I'm losing people.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:32.760
- Yeah, well I mean I think,

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.030
look, as a radio personality, I think

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.630
everybody loves to talk
shit about the station

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.830
or the distribution or
things of that nature,

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.704
but what creatives forget is that

00:04:41.704 --> 00:04:43.440
distribution's a part of it, right?

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.770
And being on Westwood One back in the day

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:51.458
or on iHeart, this,
distribution's part of it.

00:04:51.458 --> 00:04:55.970
But to me, you still have an advantage

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:56.803
of being able to

00:04:56.803 --> 00:04:58.330
no matter how much
friction you're creating

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.733
for somebody who's listening on the radio,

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:04.570
it's still more built in audience

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.960
than somebody who starts tomorrow

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.890
and is only at the mercy of winning

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:11.810
in a digital landscape.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.340
So you have an advantage,
it's just that it's

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.180
not enough an advantage
for you not to be great

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.980
at Facebook ads, at doing collaborations,

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:23.520
showing up on other people's podcasts.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.940
In today's world where everybody can win,

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.320
that means that everybody can win.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.450
Which means you have to fuckin' be better

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:34.875
than ever before to break through.

00:05:34.875 --> 00:05:35.708
- [Anthony] Right.

00:05:35.708 --> 00:05:39.320
- Everybody's got a hot take
on the Drake Pusha debate.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.300
Everybody's got a hot take on

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.090
the Golden State Warriors and LeBron.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:45.820
Everybody's got a hot take on

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:47.820
Ben Simmons and Kendall dating.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.600
Everybody's got a hot
take on Roseanne Barr.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:51.912
In that world where

00:05:51.912 --> 00:05:54.060
you're gonna have to fuckin' be better at

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.420
content and distribution.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:57.040
And so yes it is hard.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:58.220
And I always tell people, they're like,

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.053
man it's so hard.

00:05:59.053 --> 00:06:01.340
I'm like, at least you have a chance.

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:04.800
In 1982, if you weren't
picked by somebody,

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.830
and one of the 17 people
that got to have an opinion,

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:09.588
you weren't heard!

00:06:09.588 --> 00:06:11.905
- [Anthony] Exactly.

00:06:11.905 --> 00:06:13.180
And I think, you know what's funny?

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.580
'Cause I know it's switching gears but

00:06:16.439 --> 00:06:17.330
under the big umbrella I think it kinda

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:19.160
all falls under the same place.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:22.000
You talk about '80s
brands coming back, right?

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.220
- Yes.
- [Anthony] It's happening

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.030
